### Hunk 3 — §7 `[reconstructed anchor]`

```diff
@@ §7  ECHO CONTROL @@
   Structural properties that hold echo rate down:
-  · bank entries never indexed
-  · index and ordinal replies rejected
+  · bank entries never indexed
+  · entries generic enough that selection under-resolves rather
+    than misresolves
   · own-words line primary
   · [N/A] capped at twelve words
+
+  Note: ordinal rejection previously contributed here and has been
+  removed (G.3b). Echo will run higher than the pre-G.3b draft
+  assumed. What the remaining properties guarantee is that an
+  echoed value is coarse, not wrong — the accepted trade, not a
+  suppression claim.
```

### Hunk 4 — §G.3a, ordering `[reconstructed anchor]`

```diff
@@ §G.3a  RENDER @@
-      Entries are rendered vertically, unindexed, in fixed order.
+      Entries are rendered vertically, unindexed, in fixed
+      alphabetical order.
```

No-op if G.3a already specifies alphabetical. If it does not, this is required — G.3b's positional matching counts against the rendered order, and *fixed* alone permits two renders of the same bank to differ. One word makes positional matching mechanical.

### Hunk 5 — §D.9a, cross-reference gloss `[reconstructed anchor — verify wording]`

```diff
@@ §D.9a  NO NEW NOUN @@
-      (see G.3b — examples are not values)
+      (see G.3b — an example becomes a value only by user
+      selection, never by system inference)
```

The old gloss now states the inverse of the clause it cites. The replacement preserves what D.9a actually needs from G.3b: the bar on *system-side* adoption. Selection by the user was never the thing no-new-noun was guarding against.

